GETTING HELP AND READING FILES

A - Getting help and reading files

:A
GETTING HELP AND READING FILES

The HELP command is worth remembering. A copy of the essential caller
instructions is always available on the machine itself.

    HELP
    HELP MOUNT
    HELP TRANSFER
    HELP RBBS
    DIR HELP:

An unqualified HELP opens the main topic. HELP followed by a topic name opens
that topic directly. HELP // gives the preserved viewer's own usage
information.

Select a letter from a menu to open another topic or an information section.
Use ^ to return to the preceding menu and . to return to the root. Long
sections are paginated automatically; follow the viewer's prompts.

To examine an ordinary text file, try LT:

    LT A0:WELCOME.TXT

LT is also useful for reading supported compressed text and text members
inside libraries. TYPE is available for ordinary text files. CLS clears the
terminal display. DIR and SDZ list directories; SDZ is the same supplied Super
Directory utility as DIR.

Don't attempt to read a binary executable as text. Apart from being
uninformative, the output can confuse your terminal.
